<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-ha-tooltip"><code>ha-tooltip</code></h3>

A Home Assistant building block, used across its screens. Dashboard authors cannot place it, so there is no YAML for it. These are its settings, for reference:

| Setting | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `for` | The id of the control it belongs to |
| `placement` | Where it opens: `top` (the default), `right`, `bottom` or `left`, each with `-start` or `-end` |
| `trigger` | `hover focus` by default; `click` and `manual` are also possible |
| `show-delay` and `hide-delay` | 350 and 150 milliseconds by default |
| `distance` and `skidding` | The gap from the control and the sideways shift |
| `disabled` and `without-arrow` | Switch the tooltip off, or hide the arrow |

The text goes in the default slot. Interactive content in a tooltip should be avoided.

Pressing Escape closes an open tooltip. A tooltip stays open while the pointer moves from the control onto the tooltip. Its text is announced as a description of the control, so keep it short and do not repeat the visible label.

**What the Civica theme sets.** Nothing specific to tooltips. The tooltip background follows the card surface colour, which is white in light mode and the dark surface in dark mode, and the text follows `primary-text-color`. Calculated from those theme colours, the text is about 19.6 to 1 on white and about 14.9 to 1 on the dark surface. The text is 16 pixels at the theme’s normal weight.

**What stays as Home Assistant draws it.** Corners are 8 pixels, not square, because the theme does not set the corner size. The tooltip has no border and no arrow by default, and its edge is a soft shadow that the theme does not set, so it can blend into a card of the same colour. These variables change it, in a theme or with card-mod: `--ha-tooltip-background-color`, `--ha-tooltip-text-color`, `--ha-tooltip-font-size`, `--ha-tooltip-padding`, `--ha-tooltip-border-radius`, `--ha-tooltip-arrow-size` and `--ha-tooltip-box-shadow`. Changes through card-mod need testing on a real dashboard.

<h3 id="child-ha-help-tooltip"><code>ha-help-tooltip</code></h3>

A small help icon with a tooltip, used in some settings screens. It takes a `label` for the tooltip text and a `position` with the same placements as above. The icon is 14 pixels and uses the disabled text colour, which is the secondary text colour in the Civica theme. `--ha-help-tooltip-size` and `--ha-help-tooltip-color` change it. Calculated from the theme colours, the icon colour is about 9 to 1 on white and 9.6 to 1 on the dark surface.

The help icon cannot take keyboard focus, and its drawing is hidden from screen readers. The text opens only when someone hovers over it with a pointer. Keyboard and screen reader users cannot reach it. Do not put anything people need to know in a help tooltip. Write it as visible text instead, as in the example below.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A tooltip keeps a screen tidy and names an icon button where there is no room for a word.
- Escape closes it, and the pointer can move onto it without it closing.
- A hover tip cannot be relied on for essential information. A help icon cannot be reached by keyboard at all.
- The Civica theme does not restyle tooltips, so they keep rounded corners.
- A dashboard cannot add one. In a Markdown card the only hover text kept is the `title` attribute on a link or an abbreviation, which a keyboard cannot open.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Never put an instruction, a warning or the only explanation of a field in a tooltip. Write it as visible text under the label. Use a tooltip only for a short extra name on an icon button, and give that button a visible word wherever there is space. Keep tooltip text to a few words.

For help that is not essential, link to a longer explanation from visible text. A `details` section, or a link to a help page, works on every device.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.1.1 Non-text Content and 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: an icon button needs a name. See [Icon and state icon](icon-and-state-icon.html).
- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the tooltip text is announced as a description of the control, not as its name.
- 1.4.13 Content on Hover or Focus: it can be dismissed with Escape and the pointer can move onto it. Check how long it stays on your own screens.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard: the help icon cannot be reached.
- 1.4.3 and 1.4.11 Contrast: the tooltip edge is a faint shadow. Check it on your own dashboard.

Visible help written in plain words costs people less effort than a tip they have to find.

